so exactly why was quarantine imposed on  france so  suddenly well on thursday france  reported 2669  new infections in just 24 hours  that's the highest daily increase in the  country since its lockdown was lifted in  may  by comparison the uk has recorded around  1 000 infections a day  during the last week across france there  are regional differences levels of  infection  are higher in the capital paris  and its surrounding areas but also here  on the south coast  which is one of the most popular  destinations for tourists  here's how the uk was affected by the  pandemic a sharp  rise in cases leading to the peak in  april  and then declining gradually this is  where we are now  the office for national statistics says  infections are leveling off  although we know infections have risen  slightly today  and this is how the situation in france  compares to what is happening  in the uk you can see here that the two  lines follow a broadly similar pattern  but look at where they are now this  sharp increase  and now let's look at the infections in  spain it has one of the worst  coronavirus infection rates in western  europe  you can see here the infection rate  spiking and it was at the end  of july that the government imposed a  quarantine rule  on holiday makers returning to the uk  you can see a slight dip in the  infection rate  in the past few days but further  restrictions have been announced  including a ban on smoking in all public  places  and late night drinking in bars malta  and the netherlands have also been added  to the quarantine list and infections  there  are spiking faster than france the  question is  will the uk follow the same trajectory  as some of our european neighbors like  france  now they're seeing more regional more  nationwide  increases and that's what we might get  in this country we may follow the same  path we've had smaller localized  outbreaks and we've had one regional  outbreak where we've gone we've got a  restrictions in place from east  lancashire all the way through greater  manchester  and into parts of west yorkshire so  there is precedence in this country for  it happening here as well  it must be remembered that these are new  infections  and not deaths localized flare-ups were  expected  with lockdown easing we can say we are  now in a  different phase of the pandemic and  governments across  europe will be watching and reacting to  the rising  infection rates very closely
 

